  "facts": {
    "point": "§FS-remote-projects.declaration.none",
    "authoredFrom": "Read from the case's input bytes: grund.toml (version 1, `project_name = \"consumer\"`, strict, `[id] format = \"{kind}-{slug}\"`, untitled GOAL homed at docs/goals.md and FS at docs/fs.md, `[scan] include = [\"docs\", \".grund/notes.md\"]`, `[workspace] members = []`), docs/fs.md, docs/goals.md and .grund/notes.md; grund.lock and .grund/remotes/x are bytes no table asks for. With no `[workspace.remotes]` table a run reads neither the lock nor the remotes directory (§FS-remote-projects.declaration.none), so GOAL-x, declared only in the unread copy, dangles at docs/fs.md:5 and GOAL-gone at the explicitly scanned note (§FS-check.3.1); the copy's duplicate GOAL-local is no second claimant. The `[workspace]` block makes every run a workspace run whose current project is the root, `consumer` (§FS-workspace.8.9): an unqualified ID resolves there and `consumer/<ID>` is the same query (§FS-workspace.8.2.2); a run below the root outside every member loads the same workspace and narrows its report (§FS-scan.projects.narrowed). Exhaustive batch discovery spells the current project's IDs unqualified (§FS-workspace.8.1.5), list qualifies every ID (§FS-workspace.8.3.1), and the editor asks a citation's question in its own project (§FS-lsp.1.2.5). FS-a is never cited (§FS-check.4.1); GOAL-local is cited twice. Findings are in path, line, message order (§FS-errors.4.1) beside the site-less version caution (§FS-check.2.1.1). Kinds without a title carry none (§FS-config.3.4.3). Cross-reference emission wraps the two resolving GOAL-local citations in Markdown and leaves the dangling ones bare (§FS-fmt.6, §FS-fmt.6.4). FS-404 is the absent control.",
